meta {
  name: Lead Pull - List Campaigns
  type: http
  seq: 17
}

get {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/meta/lead-pull/campaigns?tenant_id={{tenantId}}&page_ids=
  body: none
  auth: none
}

headers {
  Cookie: auth_token={{authToken}}
}

docs {
  The campaign multiselect for the lead-pull screen, sourced from
  ext.meta_campaigns.

  ?page_ids= is an optional CSV of numeric Meta Page ids. It narrows the list to
  campaigns that have ALREADY DELIVERED a lead on one of those pages — an
  OBSERVED association (ext.meta_leads), not one Meta models. There is no
  page->campaign edge in the Graph API and ext.meta_campaigns has no page
  dimension at all.

  The response carries campaign_filter_is_post_fetch: true, and the UI MUST
  render it. Selecting campaigns narrows the RESULT of a pull, never the work:
  leads come page -> /{page-id}/leadgen_forms -> /{form-id}/leads and campaign_id
  is only a FIELD on each lead, so the filter is applied after every lead has
  already been paid for. Without that message the first thing an admin does is
  pick one campaign, wait exactly as long as for all of them, and file a bug.

  Requires super_admin and an explicit ?tenant_id= — never the caller's own
  tenant, since platform staff administer a tenant other than their own.

  Regression checks:
    * as tenant A, no campaign from tenant B appears. The scoping is
      ext.meta_campaigns' admin_tenant_config_policy, not a WHERE clause.
    * page_ids naming a page with no mapping row -> empty list, not every campaign
    * a bogus tenant_id                          -> 404 "Tenant not found"
    * missing tenant_id                          -> 422
    * caller below super_admin                   -> 403
}
